      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Freethinker societies kept formal churches out of Thiensville until 1919, delaying religious buildings for eight decades.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        7,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thiensville%2C_Wisconsin",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Pigeon Creek winds through downtown Thiensville, joining the Milwaukee River near the old village hall at Main and Green Bay.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thiensville%2C_Wisconsin",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A dam on the Milwaukee River sits upstream of the creek’s confluence, controlling water flow through the village.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thiensville%2C_Wisconsin",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1866, Thiensville built a larger school to accommodate the influx of immigrant families.",
